{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q009_heme_bundle_fold_not_homology","answer":"No. SCOPe does not support treating them as one homologous superfamily. In SCOPe 2.08 (stable release; the 2.08-2026-08-16 periodic update is current), all three groups sit under one fold, f.21 "Heme-binding four-helical bundle" (sunid 81344), in class f "Membrane and cell surface proteins and peptides" (sunid 56835). Each is its own superfamily: f.21.1 Transmembrane di-heme cytochromes (sunid 81342), f.21.2 Fumarate reductase respiratory complex transmembrane subunits (sunid 81343), and f.21.3 Respiratory nitrate reductase 1 gamma chain (sunid 103501). The fold node's only children are these three superfamilies. The fold annotation describes the shared architecture: four transmembrane helices in an up-and-down bundle, binding one or two hemes between the helices.

Interpretation: the shared four-helix heme-binding architecture is a fold-level (structural) similarity. In SCOPe, only family and superfamily levels carry homology assertions. The f.21 homology response says that levels above superfamily do not imply homology, and it returned no heterogeneity or repeat caveats. Because the three groups are separate superfamilies, SCOPe makes no positive claim of common ancestry among them. A shared fold, a shared heme cofactor, or a similar bundle topology does not justify merging them or calling them one homologous group.

Limits: this does not show the groups are unrelated, analogous, or convergent. Different superfamilies mean only that SCOPe asserts no superfamily-level homology. A negative evolutionary claim would need independent evidence, such as structural alignments, sequence correspondence at equivalent core positions, and phylogenetic or experimental support. I did not retrieve any of that. I also did not retrieve domain-level lineages or SIDs for particular PDB entries, so I make no per-structure claims. Do not transfer function, heme number or position, redox partner, substrate, or an interchangeable template across these superfamilies from fold membership alone. For benchmarks, treat them at most as same-fold, different-superfamily pairs, i.e. operational SCOPe-negatives with no SCOPe homology assertion rather than proven non-homologs.","evidence_used":["scop-homology/SKILL.md","scop-homology/references/api-usage.md","scop-homology/references/biological-interpretation.md","/api/v1/releases/current","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/f.21","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/f.21.1","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/f.21.2","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/f.21.3","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/f.21.1/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81344/children?limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81344/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81344/annotations"]}]}
